Changes to quic-go v0.65.0, applied by scripts/vendor-quicgo.sh (patch -p1, before the import paths are rewritten).
The patch is the only edit to the vendored code. It adds a hook for a pluggable congestion controller, the statistics kolotransport.Conn.Stats reads, a datagram send that never blocks and path MTU black hole detection, and nothing else.

  congestion/interface.go (new)          the controller interface, AckedPacketInfo and LostPacketInfo
  monotime/time.go (new)                 exports the clock type the controller interface uses
  internal/ackhandler/cc_adapter.go (new) lets a controller stand in for the built-in sender and keeps
                                         ConnectionStats (CongestionWindow, PacketsLost, BytesLost, BandwidthEstimate)
                                         current; its Name is the controller's own
  internal/ackhandler/sent_packet_handler.go
                                         every congestion call goes through getCongestionControl; the acked and lost
                                         packets of each ACK or loss timer event are passed to a controller that takes
                                         them together (a lost Path MTU probe is not a congestion loss); the controller
                                         is built by ccFactory, set with SetCongestionControlFactory, and built again
                                         after a path migration (the built-in sender would silently come back)
                                         only the packets of the application data space reach a pluggable controller (the
                                         Initial and Handshake spaces number their own packets from 0)
                                         SetCubic switches the built-in sender from Reno to Cubic (the sender's reno
                                         flag was hard-coded to true) and a migration keeps it; BytesInFlight is
                                         published to ConnectionStats at each change
  internal/ackhandler/interfaces.go      SetCongestionControlFactory, SetCubic and CongestionControlName on the sent packet handler
  internal/congestion/interface.go       SendAlgorithmEx, the controller with the event method
  internal/congestion/cubic_sender.go    Name, "reno" or "cubic"
  connection.go                          Conn.SetCongestionControlFactory, Conn.UseCubic, Conn.CongestionControlName;
                                         ConnectionStats gets HasRTT, BytesInFlight, BandwidthEstimate,
                                         MaxDatagramSize and PathChanges; Conn.TrySendDatagram, a variant of
                                         SendDatagram that drops instead of waiting while the send queue is full
                                         (SendDatagram still waits; both share datagramFrame, its checks and copy)
  datagram_queue.go                      TryAdd, Add that returns false instead of waiting for room in the 32-frame queue
  internal/utils/connstats.go            the counters behind those fields
  internal/utils/rtt_stats.go            hasMeasurement is atomic, so another goroutine can read it
  internal/ackhandler/black_hole.go (new) black hole detection (RFC 8899 section 4.3): more than 3 loss bursts of packets
                                         larger than 1200 bytes that no later acknowledged packet as large explains
  internal/ackhandler/black_hole_test.go (new)
                                         its tests, and those of the sent packet handler's reports
  internal/ackhandler/sent_packet_handler.go, interfaces.go (black hole)
                                         feeds the detector the acknowledged and lost 1-RTT packets, reports 3 probe
                                         timeouts in a row with a packet larger than 1200 bytes outstanding (RFC 4821
                                         section 7.7), resets it on a path migration; BlackHoleDetected reports once and
                                         starts over, ignoring the packets sent before the report
  packet_packer.go                       drops a queued DATAGRAM frame larger than a whole packet, even when the packet
                                         carries an ACK, so it does not hold back the queue after a fall back
  packet_packer_datagram_test.go (new)   its test
  connection.go (black hole)             handleBlackHole, after each ACK and loss timer: the packet size, the datagram
                                         limit and the congestion controller's datagram size fall back to 1200 bytes
                                         and path MTU discovery starts again

